      "title": "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bcfd1634-08da-57da-80b7-34f3a4e0e62c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $x$ given a fixed value of $y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/12eea222f88dcf3ad5dc0973ef535e168e0133a71fe6f7e9db76ae8138a6e4d7.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/98d0deb6f74dc9595aacc08aa2772db455f5e0722e0c2af137bb115a3b73b43d.png)",
